  "content": {
    "id": "v2-a9dca-p13-practice-slot-7-lower-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "If the length of each side of a square is increased by 5, the area is multiplied by 9.\nFind the length of one side of the original square.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\dfrac{5}{2}$ units.",
      "worked": "Let $x$ units be the original side length, with $x>0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\n(x+5)^2 &= 9x^2 \\\\\nx^2+10x+25 &= 9x^2 \\\\\n8x^2-10x-25 &= 0 \\\\\n8x^2-20x+10x-25 &= 0 \\\\\n4x(2x-5)+5(2x-5) &= 0 \\\\\n(4x+5)(2x-5) &= 0\n\\end{align*}$$\nP: $-200$; S: $-10$; F: $-20,10$.\nBy the null factor law, $x=-\\dfrac{5}{4}$ or $x=\\dfrac{5}{2}$.\nReject the negative length. The original side length is $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ units.\nCheck: the new side length is $\\dfrac{15}{2}$ units, so the area multiplier is $\\dfrac{(\\frac{15}{2})^2}{(\\frac{5}{2})^2}=9$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": null
  },
